{
  "id": "cb4",
  "title": "Decimals and Signs",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "cb4-l1",
      "title": "V: The Invisible Point",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Money has cents. PIC 9(3)V99 holds 3 digits before the decimal point and 2 after it: up to 999.99. The V marks where the point is.",
        "But the point itself is never stored: V is invisible. The field holds just the 5 digits, so DISPLAY shows 01250 for 12.50. On punched cards and tapes, every character saved cost money.",
        "To print a real point, COBOL has edited pictures, in the next unit. For calculating, V is what you use."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
          "       PROGRAM-ID. PRICES.",
          "       DATA DIVISION.",
          "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
          "       01 PRICE     PIC 9(3)V99 VALUE 12.50.",
          "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
          "           ADD 0.75 TO PRICE.",
          "           DISPLAY \"PRICE: \" PRICE.",
          "           MOVE 2.999 TO PRICE.",
          "           DISPLAY \"PRICE: \" PRICE.",
          "           STOP RUN."
        ],
        "output": [
          "PRICE: 01325",
          "PRICE: 00299"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "cb4-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this job show?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
            "       PROGRAM-ID. V.",
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 X PIC 9(2)V9 VALUE 4.5.",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           DISPLAY X.",
            "           STOP RUN."
          ],
          "options": [
            "045",
            "4.5",
            "04.5",
            "45"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "PIC 9(2)V9 has 3 digits: 04 and 5. The point isn't shown."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many digits come after the point in PIC 9(4)V99?",
          "options": [
            "2",
            "4",
            "6",
            "99"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The two 9s after the V."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l1-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Declare a price with 2 decimals.",
          "code": [
            "       01 PRICE PIC 9(3)___99."
          ],
          "options": [
            "V",
            ".",
            ",",
            "P"
          ],
          "answer": "V",
          "explanation": "V marks the decimal point inside the number."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why is the point invisible (V) in the stored number?",
          "options": [
            "To save space: only the digits are stored",
            "Because COBOL can't print points",
            "To hide prices",
            "It's a mistake"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The program knows where the point is, so it doesn't need to store it."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l1-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This job doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
