    {
      "id": "cb4-l2",
      "title": "S: Negative Numbers",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A plain PIC 9 field has no sign: it can't be negative. If a calculation goes below zero, the sign is simply lost. A balance of -150 becomes 150, a classic way to give customers free money.",
        "PIC S9(4) has a sign. The S takes no extra place: on the mainframe, the sign is stored in the last digit. This app shows a negative field with a - after the digits.",
        "Rule of thumb: every field that can go below zero, like a balance or a difference, needs an S."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
          "       PROGRAM-ID. BALANCE.",
          "       DATA DIVISION.",
          "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
          "       01 BALANCE   PIC S9(4) VALUE 100.",
          "       01 WRONG     PIC 9(4)  VALUE 100.",
          "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
          "           ADD -250 TO BALANCE WRONG.",
          "           DISPLAY \"WITH S:    \" BALANCE.",
          "           DISPLAY \"WITHOUT S: \" WRONG.",
          "           STOP RUN."
        ],
        "output": [
          "WITH S:    0150-",
          "WITHOUT S: 0150"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "cb4-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does the S in PIC S9(5) do?",
          "options": [
            "Gives the field a sign, so it can be negative",
            "Makes it a string",
            "Makes it smaller",
            "Adds a space"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "S for sign."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this job show?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
            "       PROGRAM-ID. NOSIGN.",
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 N PIC 9(4).",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           MOVE -7 TO N.",
            "           DISPLAY N.",
            "           STOP RUN."
          ],
          "options": [
            "0007",
            "0007-",
            "-0007",
            "an error"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The field has no S, so -7 loses its sign."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l2-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Declare a balance that can go below zero.",
          "code": [
            "       01 BALANCE PIC ___9(5)V99 VALUE ZERO."
          ],
          "options": [
            "S",
            "-",
            "+",
            "N"
          ],
          "answer": "S",
          "explanation": "S comes first."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this job show here?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
            "       PROGRAM-ID. SIGN.",
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 N PIC S9(4).",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           MOVE -7 TO N.",
            "           DISPLAY N.",
            "           STOP RUN."
          ],
          "options": [
            "0007-",
            "0007",
            "-7",
            "an error"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "With S, the sign is kept. This app shows it as a - after the digits."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l2-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "A customer with 50 dollars spends 80. The job reports a balance of 30. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
            "       PROGRAM-ID. SPEND.",
            "       DATA DIVISION.",
            "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
            "       01 BALANCE PIC 9(4) VALUE 50.",
            "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
            "           ADD -80 TO BALANCE.",
            "           DISPLAY BALANCE.",
            "           STOP RUN."
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "BALANCE has no S, so -30 loses its sign and looks like +30. Use PIC S9(4)."
        },
        {
          "id": "cb4-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many characters does PIC S9(4) take?",
          "options": [
            "4: the sign lives in the last digit",
            "5",
            "6",
            "1"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Unlike a printed minus, the S costs no extra place."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cb4-l3",
      "title": "Exact Money",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "In binary, 0.10 can't be stored exactly. FORTRAN's REAL numbers add up tiny errors, and 10,000 × 0.10 isn't quite 1,000. For a bank, being off by a cent is not an option.",
        "COBOL calculates in decimal, digit by digit, exactly like on paper. 0.10 added ten times is exactly 1.00, every time.",
        "The price for that: you choose the size. PIC 9(3)V99 can't go above 999.99, and anything bigger loses its front digits."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
          "       PROGRAM-ID. CENTS.",
          "       DATA DIVISION.",
          "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
          "       01 TOTAL     PIC 9(3)V99 VALUE ZERO.",
          "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
          "           ADD 0.10 0.10 0.10 0.10 0.10 TO TOTAL.",
          "           ADD 0.10 0.10 0.10 0.10 0.10 TO TOTAL.",
          "           DISPLAY \"TEN DIMES: \" TOTAL.",
          "           ADD 999 TO TOTAL.",
          "           DISPLAY \"TOO BIG:   \" TOTAL.",
          "           STOP RUN."
        ],
        "output": [
          "TEN DIMES: 00100",
          "TOO BIG:   00000"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "cb4-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why do banks like COBOL's arithmetic?",
          "options": [
            "It works in decimal, so cents are always exact",
            "It's faster than binary",
            "It rounds everything up",
            "It has no limits"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The same result as on paper, every time."
        },
